ly:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;11.3&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #000;border-top:1pt solid #929292;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:5pt;padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The risk management assets listed in the tables above include FTRs used to manage transmission congestion costs in the MISO market. See Note 2 , &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;&#8220;Risk Management Activities,&#8221;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; for more information on derivative instruments. There were no transfers between the levels of the fair value hierarchy during 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;This table is a reconciliation of changes in the fair value of FTRs categorized as Level 3 measurements:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:18pt;padding-right:18pt;text-align:center"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:59.077%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:56.078%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:20.310%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:20.312%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;(Thousands)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:36pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:112%"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:36pt;text-align:right"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;border-top:1pt solid #929292;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#323232;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Balance at Beginning of Period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:0.5pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;text-indent:10pt;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:700;line-height:112%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:0.5pt solid #000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:700;line-height:112%"&gt;11.4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:0.5pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:0.5pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;text-indent:10pt;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:0.5pt solid #000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;44.0&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-top:0.5pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#323232;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Net unrealized gains&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:700;line-height:112%"&gt;11.2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;7.2&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#323232;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Purchases&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:700;line-height:112%"&gt;39.9&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;52.3&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#323232;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Settlements&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:700;line-height:112%"&gt;(49.5)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;(92.1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;border-bottom:1pt solid #929292;border-top:1pt solid #929292;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#212121;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;Balance at &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;E&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#212121;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;nd of Period&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:0.5pt solid #000;border-top:0.25pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;text-indent:10.05pt;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:700;line-height:112%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:0.5pt solid #000;border-top:0.25pt solid #000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:700;line-height:112%"&gt;13.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:0.5pt solid #000;border-top:0.25pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:0.5pt solid #000;border-top:0.25pt solid #000;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;text-indent:10.05pt;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:0.5pt solid #000;border-top:0.25pt solid #000;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:9.5pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;11.4&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="border-bottom:0.5pt solid #000;border-top:0.25pt solid #000;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:top"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:5pt;padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Unrealized gains and losses on FTR's are deferred as regulatory assets or liabilities. Therefore, these fair value measurements have no impact on earnings. Realized gains and losses on FTR's, as well as the related transmission congestion costs are recorded in &#8221;operating expenses&#8221; on the Statement of Income.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:36pt;text-indent:-36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%;padding-left:21.93pt"&gt;REGULATORY ENVIRONMENT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2024 Rate Review Proceeding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 21, 2024 UPPCO filed, in Case No. U-21555, a request to increase its retail electric rates by $16,913 annually. This filing is based upon a 12-month projected test year ending December 31, 2025. For the projected test year, excluding the impacts of previously approved revenue offsets of approximately $985.5, the Company proposed a jurisdictional rate relief totaling $15,928.4.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The requested increase reflected a 10.70% return on common equity with a regulatory capital structure reflecting a common equity ratio of 51.52%. Key factors contributing to the rate filing included continued infrastructure investments and associated operating expenditures needed to improve the reliability and resiliency of UPPCO&#8217;s distribution system, and general inflation and supply chain pressures.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;On September 26, 2024, MPSC Order No. U-21555 approved a settlement agreement resolving all issues in the rate case. Based on a 12-month projected test year ending December 31, 2025, the Company will adjust rates by $9,000.0 beginning on January 1, 2025. The settlement agreement reflects a 9.86% return on common equity with a regulatory capital structure reflecting a common equity ratio of 50.0% and a jurisdictional rate base value of $372,100.0.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2022 Rate Review Proceeding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On September 8, 2022, in Case No. U-21286, UPPCO filed with the Michigan Public Service Commission (MPSC), a request to increase its retail electric rates by $25,300.0 annually. The filing was based upon a 12-month projected test year ending June 30, 2024. For the projected test year, excluding the impacts of previously ordered revenue offsets of approximately $8,000.0, the Company proposed jurisdictional rate relief totaling $17,300.0.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The requested increase reflected a 10.80% return on common equity with a regulatory capital structure reflecting a common equity ratio of 53.96%. Key factors contributing to the rate filing include continued infrastructure investments and associated operating expenditures needed to improve the reliability and resiliency of UPPCO&#8217;s distribution system. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 24, 2023, MPSC Order No. U-21286 approved a settlement agreement resolving all issues in the rate case. Based on a 12-month projected test year ending June 30, 2024, the Company will adjusted rates by $10,800.0 beginning on July 1, 2023. The settlement agreement reflected a 9.9% return on common equity with a regulatory capital structure reflecting a common equity ratio of 51.5% and a jurisdictional rate base value of $334,300.0. Among other things, as part of the approved settlement agreement, the Company was authorized to establish a regulatory asset of $863.1 associated with deferred bad debt expense. UPPCO reduced this amount by $200.0 and donated $200.0 to organizations benefiting low-income customers in UPPCO's service territory. The remaining balance of the regulatory asset of $663.1 was amortized over a two-year period ending June 30, 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2023 Depreciation Rate Review&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On December 6, 2023 UPPCO filed, in Case No. U-21513, a request for accounting approval of proposed new depreciation rates that would result in a decrease in annual depreciation rates.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The proposed rates reflect a downward adjustment in depreciation expense for the Company when compared to the depreciation rates approved in its prior depreciation rate review, Case No. U-18467. On May 23, 2024, the MPSC issued an order approving a settlement agreement that resolves all issues in the case. Through this order, the Commission directed UPPCO to implement the new depreciation rates at the time that new rates for general service are implemented following the Company&#8217;s 2024 Rate &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;Review Proceeding, Case No. U-21555. The rates from both of these proceedings took effect on January 1, 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:112%"&gt;2017 Tax Cuts and Jobs Act &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On December 27, 2017, the MPSC issued an order to consider changes in the rates of all Michigan rate-regulated utilities to reflect the effects of the federal TCJA. On January 19, 2018, UPPCO filed information with the MPSC regarding the potential change in revenue requirements due to the TCJA effective January 1, 2018 and outlined UPPCO&#8217;s recommended method to flow the current and deferred tax benefits of those impacts to ratepayers. UPPCO estimated a reduction in its revenue requirement of $3,800 and one-time reduction to its deferred tax balance of $4,600.0 related to regulated operations, because of the reduced federal tax rate. In its February 22, 2018 Order, the MPSC ordered that utilities, including UPPCO, implement a three-step approach to addressing the tax law change &#8211; Credit A, Credit B and Calculation C. Credit A was a &#8220;going-forward&#8221; tax credit addressing the impact of tax reform on the revenue requirement for 2018, which was based on UPPCO&#8217;s most recent previously approved revenue requirement in 2016, for amounts subject to refund for customers. Credit B was a &#8220;backward-looking&#8221; tax credit addressing the period of January 1, 2018 to the date that Credit A was implemented, but was otherwise identical to Credit A. Calculation C addressed excess deferred taxes, bonus depreciation and other impacts not captured by Credits A and B. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Credits A and B were fully resolved and refunded to customers prior to 2020. Calculation C was resolved via the settlement received in Case No. U-20276 on May 23, 2019 and is reflected in base rates as&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;outlined in the settlement agreement. UPPCO amortized the regulatory liability established for the Calculation C as a benefit to customers as a reduction of income tax expense of ($4,692.3) over the five year period of 2019 to 2024, or ($938.5) per year.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;2019 Integrated Resource Plan (&#8220;IRP&#8221;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On February 6, 2020, the MPSC approved a settlement agreement in the matter of the application of UPPCO for approval of the Company&#8217;s IRP pursuant to MCL 460.6t in Case No. U-20350. Through approval of the settlement agreement, the Commission determined that the settlement agreement was in the public interest and represented a fair and reasonable resolution to the proceeding. The key terms of the settlement agreement approving a 125 Megawatt (MW) Solar Purchased Power Agreement (&#8220;Solar PPA&#8221;), as well as, two 20 MW Wind Purchased Power Agreements (&#8220;Wind PPAs&#8221;), outlined that UPPCO shall build 62.5 MW of company-owned generation and procure 22.5 MW of renewable PPA in the event that the Solar PPA is cancelled. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;On February 21, 2020, UPPCO and a third party reached a mutual understanding that the Solar PPA automatically terminated on January 8, 2020. Pursuant to the Commission Order approving the settlement agreement, UPPCO issued a Request for Proposal (RFP) for 62.5 MW of company-owned solar and 22.5 MW of renewable PPA in October of 2020. On May 20, 2024, UPPCO purchased Republic Solar for $2,761.5, and intends to continue development of the project, with an expected in-service date of Q4 2027. On May 21, 2024, UPPCO signed a PPA with Copper Country Power LLC wherein a 62.5MW solar electric generating facility known as Groveland Mine Solar Project, is under development in Dickinson County, MI. The Groveland Mine Solar Project is expected to be in-service by Q4 2028. The MPSC approved the PPA on August 22, 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;On November 21, 2024 UPPCO filed an application seeking MPSC approval of two Engineering, Procurement, and Construction contracts necessary to install and commission the Republic Solar Project, siting an anticipated total project cost of $154,900.0.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;On June 12, 2025 the Commission approved the Member Interest Purchase Agreement between&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;Upper Peninsula Power Company and Republic Solar Power Holdings, LLC, as well as the two&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;Engineering, Procurement, and Construction Agreements for the construction of the Republic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;Solar Project, and related accounting authority. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:112%"&gt;2025 Integrated Resource Plan (&#8220;IRP&#8221;) Filing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;UPPCO is required to file a new IRP, pursuant to MCL 460.6t, with the Michigan Public Service Commission no later than March 6, 2025, UPPCO completed this filing on February 6, 2025. This analysis contained within this filing will be consistent with the attributes of the new energy Michigan energy legislation, namely Public Act 235 and Public Act 231, which took effect on February 13, 2024.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;The key components of UPPCO&#8217;s PCA are:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:72pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;a.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%;padding-left:9.97pt"&gt;15-year power purchase agreement for 37.5MW of installed solar capacity, relating to the Groveland Mine Solar facility.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:72pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;b.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%;padding-left:9.46pt"&gt;15-year power purchase agreement for 1.0MW of installed hydroelectric capacity, relating to the Cataract Hydroelectric facility.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:72pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;c.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%;padding-left:10.58pt"&gt;Approval of the application of a financial compensation mechanism applying to all qualifying power purchase agreements, consistent with MCL 460.6t(15).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:72pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;d.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%;padding-left:9.46pt"&gt;Interconnection of the existing Escanaba River hydroelectric facilities to UPPCO&#8217;s distribution system, and the inclusion of these assets within UPPCO&#8217;s rate base.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:72pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;e.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%;padding-left:9.76pt"&gt;Increase energy waste reduction savings targets to 2.17% annually, or whichever savings target can be reasonably and prudently cost-justified by the end of UPPCO&#8217;s next four-year energy waste reduction plan filing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:72pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;f.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%;padding-left:11.88pt"&gt;Identification and approval of incremental renewable generation resource additions that are necessary to meet the requirements of the Renewable Energy Standard and Clean Energy Standards, both of which are identified by PA 235 of 2023.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:72pt;text-indent:-18pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;g.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%;padding-left:10.06pt"&gt;Establishment of a regulatory asset which provides for the full recovery of all IRP-related costs, pursuant to MCL 460.6t and all other applicable laws and MPSC orders.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:112%"&gt;On October 9, 2025, the MPSC approved a settlement agreement in the matter of the application of UPPCO for approval of the Company&#8217;s IRP pursuant to MCL 460.6t in Case No. U-21809. Through approval of the settlement agreement, the Commission determined that the settlement agreement was in the public interest and represented a fair and reasonable resolution to the proceeding. The key terms of the settlement agreement included approval of all key components of UPPCO's PCA, with the exception of item (d.), the interconnection of the existing Escanaba River hydroelectric facilities to UPPCO's distribution system, and the inclusion of these assets within UPPCO's rate base. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;COVID-19, Case No. U-20757&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On April 15, 2020, the MPSC initiated proceedings in Case No. U-20757 to address pressing issues related to utility service across the state because of the novel coronavirus COVID-19. In its April 15, 2020 Order, the MPSC outlined steps it has taken in response to COVID-19 and directed additional actions to protect the public and ensure continuity of service. In its April 15, 2020 Order, the MPSC authorized rate regulated electric utilities to defer uncollectible or bad debt expense in excess of the amount used to set current rates incurred on or after March 24, 2020. As such, UPPCO tracked all COVID-19 related costs, as well as other associated extraordinary costs and impacts in anticipation of justifying cost recovery of these items in a future regulatory proceeding. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In accordance with and in response to the &#8220;Stay Home, Stay Safe&#8221; orders issued by the State of Michigan, UPPCO formally filed two affidavits in Case No. U-20757 affirming compliance with these orders which avail certain protections for the Company&#8217;s various customer groups on April 20, 2020 and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;on May 28, 2020. More specifically, these protections included (1) temporary suspension of disconnections and late payment charges for low-income customers and seniors, as well as customers medically affected by COVID-19, (2) temporary waiver of deposits and reconnection fees for customers seeking restoration of service while enduring economic hardship due to COVID-19, and (3) offering of certain financial and customer assistance in the form of flexible payment plans while continuing to direct customers to various social service organizations providing assistance to customers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Case No. U-20757 docket is closed; however, pursuant to the proposed settlement agreement in Case No. U-21286 filed for Commission review and final ruling, the Company was authorized to establish a regulatory asset of $863.1. UPPCO reduced this amount by $200.0 and donated $200.0 to organizations benefiting low-income customers in UPPCO's service territory. The remaining balance of the regulatory asset of $663.1 was amortized over a two-year period ending June 30, 2025. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:36pt;text-indent:-36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%;padding-left:21.93pt"&gt;NEW ACCOUNTING PRONOUNCEMENTS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-top:10pt;padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;I&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;ncome Taxes:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;ASU 2023&#8209;09, Incomes Taxes (Topic 740): Improvements to Income Tax Disclosures: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="padding-left:36pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Calibri',sans-serif;font-size:11pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;This standard, which is effective on January 1, 2026 for Non-Public Business Entities, was issued to enhance the transparency and decision usefulness of income tax disclosures. The amendments in this update require enhanced income tax disclosures, particularly related to a reporting entity's effective tax rate reconciliation and income taxes paid. UPPCO as a non-Public Business Entity will be required to provide a Qualitative discussion of the nature and effect of significant differences between the statutory income tax rate and the effective tax rate by specific categories of reconciling items and by individual jurisdictions (the specific categories of reconciling items used for this purpose are the same as those used by PBEs in the tabular reconciliation). UPPCO will apply the guidance upon the effective date.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock>
  <ferc:RespondentLegalName id="f-83" contextRef="c-1">Upper Peninsula Power Company</ferc:RespondentLegalName>
